## ---
   private: true
   ---
   This type is a date-time object that represents a time, often viewed as
   hour-minute-second.
   Time is represented to nanosecond precision. For example, the value
   "13:45.30.123456789" can be stored in a `Time_Of_Day`.
@Builtin_Type
type Time_Of_Day
    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       Obtains the current time from the system clock in the default time-zone.

       ## Examples
       ### The time now
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.now
       ```
    now -> Time_Of_Day = Date_Helpers.time_now

    ## ---
       group: Constants
       icon: time
       arguments:
         hour: always
         minute: always
       ---
       Constructs a new `Time_Of_Day` from an hour, minute, second and fractions
       of a second.

       Returns a `Time_Error` if the provided time is not valid.

       ## Arguments
       - `hour`: The hour-of-day to represent, from 0 to 23.
       - `minute`: The minute-of-hour to represent, from 0 to 59.
       - `second`: The second-of-minute to represent, from 0 to 59.
       - `millisecond`: The millisecond-of-second to represent, from 0 to 999.
       - `microsecond`: The microsecond-of-millisecond to represent, from 0 to
         999.
       - `nanosecond`: The nanosecond-of-microsecond to represent, from 0 to
         999.

       ## Examples
       ### A time from its hour and minute
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9 30
       # → 09:30:00
       ```

       ### Midnight
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new
       # → 00:00:00
       ```

       ### A time with a fraction of a second
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 12 34 56 millisecond=789
       # → 12:34:56.789
       ```
    @hour (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=23)
    @minute (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=59)
    @second (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=59)
    @millisecond (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=999)
    @microsecond (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=999)
    @nanosecond (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=999)
    new (hour : Integer = 0) (minute : Integer = 0) (second : Integer = 0) (millisecond : Integer = 0) (microsecond : Integer = 0) (nanosecond : Integer = 0) -> Time_Of_Day ! Time_Error =
        total_nanoseconds = nanosecond + microsecond * 1000 + millisecond * 1000000
        Panic.catch JException (Date_Helpers.time_new_builtin hour minute second total_nanoseconds) caught_panic->
            Error.throw (Time_Error.Error caught_panic.payload.getMessage)

    ## ---
       group: Conversions
       icon: convert
       arguments:
         format: always
       ---
       Converts text containing a time of day, such as "10:15", into a
       `Time_Of_Day`.

       Returns a `Time_Error` if the provided `text` cannot be parsed using the
       provided `format`.

       ## Arguments
       - `text`: The text to try and parse as a time of day.
       - `format`: A pattern describing how to parse the text, or a
         `Date_Time_Formatter`. By default the text is read as an ISO-8601 time.

       ## Examples
       ### Parse an ISO time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.parse "15:05:30"
       # → 15:05:30
       ```

       ### Parse a time with a custom format
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.parse "4:30AM" "h:mma"
       # → 04:30:00
       ```

       ### Fall back to a default time when the text is not a time
       ```
       parsed = Time_Of_Day.parse "half twelve"
       time = parsed.catch Any _-> Time_Of_Day.new 12 30
       # → 12:30:00
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### Default Time Format
       Unless you provide a custom format, the text must represent a valid time
       and is parsed using the ISO-8601 extended local time format.
       The format consists of:
       - Two digits for the hour-of-day. This is pre-padded by zero to ensure two
       digits.
       - A colon  - Two digits for the minute-of-hour. This is pre-padded by zero
       to ensure two digits.
       - If the second-of-minute is not available then the format is complete.
       - A colon  - Two digits for the second-of-minute. This is pre-padded by
       zero to ensure two digits.
       - If the nano-of-second is zero or not available then the format is
       complete.
       - A decimal point  - One to nine digits for the nano-of-second. As many
       digits will be output as required.

       ### Pattern Syntax
       If the pattern is provided as `Text`, it is parsed using the format
       described below. See `Date_Time_Formatter` for more options.
       - H: 24h hour of day (0-23).
       - h: 12h hour of day (0-12). The `a` pattern is needed to disambiguate
       between AM and PM.
       - m: Minute of hour.
       - s: Second of minute.
       - f: Fractional part of the second. The number of pattern letters
       determines the number of digits. If one letter is used, any number of
       digits will be accepted.
       - a: AM/PM marker.
    @format make_time_format_selector
    parse (text : Text) (format : Date_Time_Formatter = Date_Time_Formatter.iso_time) -> Time_Of_Day ! Time_Error =
        format.parse_time text

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The hour of this time of day, from 0 to 23.

       ## Examples
       ### The hour of a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       hour = time.hour
       # → 13
       ```
    hour self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.time_hour self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The minute of the hour of this time of day, from 0 to 59.

       ## Examples
       ### The minute of a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       minute = time.minute
       # → 45
       ```
    minute self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.time_minute self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The second of the minute of this time of day, from 0 to 59.

       ## Examples
       ### The second of a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       second = time.second
       # → 30
       ```
    second self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.time_second self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The millisecond of the second of this time of day, from 0 to 999.

       ## Examples
       ### The millisecond of a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30 123 456 789
       millisecond = time.millisecond
       # → 123
       ```
    millisecond self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.time_millisecond self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The microsecond of the millisecond of this time of day, from 0 to 999.

       ## Examples
       ### The microsecond of a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30 123 456 789
       microsecond = time.microsecond
       # → 456
       ```
    microsecond self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.time_microsecond self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The nanosecond part of this time of day.

       ## Arguments
       - `include_milliseconds`: Whether to return the whole fractional part of
         the second as nanoseconds. By default only the nanosecond of the
         microsecond is returned, from 0 to 999.

       ## Examples
       ### The nanosecond of a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30 123 456 789
       nanosecond = time.nanosecond
       # → 789
       ```

       ### The whole fraction of the second in nanoseconds
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30 123 456 789
       nanosecond = time.nanosecond include_milliseconds=True
       # → 123456789
       ```
    nanosecond self (include_milliseconds : Boolean = False) -> Integer =
        Date_Helpers.time_nanoseconds self include_milliseconds

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       The first time within the `Time_Period` that contains this time.

       ## Arguments
       - `period`: The period to find the start of. By default it is the hour.

       ## Examples
       ### The start of the hour
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       start = time.start_of
       # → 13:00:00
       ```

       ### The start of the minute
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       start = time.start_of ..Minute
       # → 13:45:00
       ```
    start_of self (period : Time_Period = ..Hour) -> Time_Of_Day = period.adjust_start self

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       The last time within the `Time_Period` that contains this time.

       ## Arguments
       - `period`: The period to find the end of. By default it is the hour.

       ## Examples
       ### The end of the hour
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       end = time.end_of
       # → 13:59:59.999999999
       ```

       ### The end of the minute
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       end = time.end_of ..Minute
       # → 13:45:59.999999999
       ```
    end_of self (period : Time_Period = ..Hour) -> Time_Of_Day = period.adjust_end self

    ## ---
       group: Conversions
       icon: convert
       ---
       The number of whole seconds since midnight, from 0 to 86,399.

       ## Examples
       ### The seconds since midnight
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       seconds = time.to_seconds
       # → 49530
       ```
    to_seconds self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.time_to_seconds self

    ## ---
       group: Conversions
       icon: convert
       ---
       Combines this time of day with a date to create a point in time.

       ## Arguments
       - `date`: The date on which this time should occur.
       - `zone`: The time zone in which the time is specified. By default it is
         the system time zone.

       ## Examples
       ### Half past noon on a date, UTC
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 12 30
       date_time = time.to_date_time (Date.new 2020 1 1) Time_Zone.utc
       # → 2020-01-01 12:30:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    @zone Time_Zone.default_widget
    to_date_time self (date : Date) (zone : Time_Zone = Time_Zone.system) -> Date_Time =
        Time_Utils.make_zoned_date_time date self zone

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       A part of this time of day, as a number.

       ## Arguments
       - `period`: The part to get: the hour, minute, second, millisecond,
         microsecond or nanosecond.

       ## Examples
       ### The minute of a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45 30
       minute = time.date_part ..Minute
       # → 45
       ```
    date_part self (period : Time_Period) -> Integer =
        case period of
            Time_Period.Hour        -> self.hour
            Time_Period.Minute      -> self.minute
            Time_Period.Second      -> self.second
            Time_Period.Millisecond -> self.millisecond
            Time_Period.Microsecond -> self.microsecond
            Time_Period.Nanosecond  -> self.nanosecond

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       The number of whole periods between this time of day and `end`.

       The result is positive if `end` is later than this time, and negative if
       it is earlier.

       ## Arguments
       - `end`: The time of day to count to.
       - `period`: The unit to count in.

       ## Examples
       ### The hours between two times
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       hours = time.date_diff (Time_Of_Day.new 17 30) ..Hour
       # → 8
       ```

       ### The minutes between two times
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       minutes = time.date_diff (Time_Of_Day.new 17 30) ..Minute
       # → 510
       ```
    date_diff self (end : Time_Of_Day) (period : Time_Period) -> Integer =
        Time_Utils.unit_time_difference period.to_java_unit self end

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       Shifts this time of day by a number of periods.

       The time wraps around midnight.

       ## Arguments
       - `amount`: The number of periods to shift by. A negative number shifts
         back.
       - `period`: The unit to shift in.

       ## Examples
       ### Shift a time forward past midnight
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 23 30
       later = time.date_add 45 ..Minute
       # → 00:15:00
       ```
    date_add self (amount : Integer) (period : Time_Period) -> Time_Of_Day =
        Time_Utils.unit_time_add period.to_java_unit self amount

    ## ---
       aliases: [add duration]
       group: Operators
       icon: time
       ---
       Adds an amount of time to this time of day to get another time of day.

       The time wraps around midnight.

       ## Arguments
       - `amount`: The amount of time to add: a `Time_Period`, or a `Duration`
         for a custom number of hours, minutes and seconds.

       ## Examples
       ### Add a duration to a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       later = time + (Duration.new minutes=90)
       # → 10:30:00
       ```

       ### Add an hour to a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       later = time + Time_Period.Hour
       # → 10:00:00
       ```
    @amount make_time_period_selector
    + self (amount : Duration | Time_Period) -> Time_Of_Day ! Time_Error =
        case amount of
            duration : Duration -> Date_Helpers.time_plus self duration
            time_period : Time_Period ->
                self + time_period.to_duration

    ## ---
       aliases: [subtract duration]
       group: Operators
       icon: time
       ---
       Subtracts an amount of time from this time of day to get another time of
       day.

       The time wraps around midnight.

       ## Arguments
       - `amount`: The amount of time to subtract: a `Time_Period`, or a
         `Duration` for a custom number of hours, minutes and seconds.

       ## Examples
       ### Subtract 12 hours from a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       earlier = time - (Duration.new hours=12)
       # → 21:00:00
       ```

       ### Subtract a minute from a time
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       earlier = time - Time_Period.Minute
       # → 08:59:00
       ```
    @amount make_time_period_selector
    - self (amount : Duration | Time_Period) -> Time_Of_Day ! Time_Error =
        case amount of
            duration : Duration -> Date_Helpers.time_minus self duration
            time_period : Time_Period ->
                self - time_period.to_duration

    ## ---
       private: true
       ---
       Convert to a JavaScript Object representing this Time_Of_Day.

       ## Examples
       ### Convert the current time to a JS_Object.

       ```
             example_to_json = Time_Of_Day.now.to_js_object
       ```
    to_js_object self -> JS_Object =
        type_pair = ["type", "Time_Of_Day"]
        cons_pair = ["constructor", "new"]
        JS_Object.from_pairs [type_pair, cons_pair, ["hour", self.hour], ["minute", self.minute], ["second", self.second], ["nanosecond", self.nanosecond include_milliseconds=True]]

    ## ---
       private: true
       ---
       Convert to a display representation of this Time_Of_Day.
    to_display_text self -> Text =
        if self.nanosecond include_milliseconds=True == 0 then self.format "HH:mm:ss" else
            self.format "HH:mm:ss.ffffff"

    ## ---
       group: Conversions
       icon: convert
       arguments:
         format: always
       ---
       Formats this time of day as text using the provided format specifier.

       ## Arguments
       - `format`: A pattern describing how to format the text, or a
         `Date_Time_Formatter`.

       ## Examples
       ### Format a time with a 24-hour clock
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 16 21 10
       text = time.format "HH:mm:ss"
       # → "16:21:10"
       ```

       ### Format a time with a 12-hour clock
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 16 21 10
       text = time.format "h:mma"
       # → "4:21PM"
       ```

       ### Format a time with a zero-padded 12-hour clock
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 16 21 10
       text = time.format "hh:mm:ssa"
       # → "04:21:10PM"
       ```

       ### Format a time with a fraction of a second
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 16 21 10 123
       text = time.format "HH:mm:ss.fff"
       # → "16:21:10.123"
       ```

       ### Format a time with literal text
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 16 21 10
       text = time.format "'hour:'h"
       # → "hour:4"
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### Pattern Syntax
       If the pattern is provided as `Text`, it is parsed using the format
       described below. See `Date_Time_Formatter` for more options.
       - H: 24h hour of day (0-23).
       - h: 12h hour of day (0-12). The `a` pattern is needed to disambiguate
       between AM and PM.
       - m: Minute of hour.
       - s: Second of minute.
       - f: Fractional part of the second. The number of pattern letters
       determines the number of digits. If one letter is used, any number of
       digits will be accepted.
       - a: AM/PM marker.
    @format make_time_format_selector
    format self (format : Date_Time_Formatter) -> Text =
        format.format_time self

    ## ---
       private: true
       group: convert
       icon: enso_logo
       ---
       Convert the value to a corresponding Enso code representation.

       ## Examples
       ### Getting the Enso code of the time 12:34:00.5

       ```
             (Time_Of_Day.new 12 34 0 500).pretty
       ```
       Returns a Text

                Time_Of_Day.new 12 34 millisecond=500
    pretty self -> Text =
        parts = Vector.build builder->
            builder.append "Time_Of_Day.new"
            if self.hour != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=1 then " hour=" else " ") + self.hour.to_text)
            if self.minute != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=2 then " minute=" else " ") + self.minute.to_text)
            if self.second != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=3 then " second=" else " ") + self.second.to_text)
            if self.millisecond != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=4 then " millisecond=" else " ") + self.millisecond.to_text)
            if self.microsecond != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=5 then " microsecond=" else " ") + self.microsecond.to_text)
            if self.nanosecond != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=6 then " nanosecond=" else " ") + self.nanosecond.to_text)
        parts.join ""

    ## ---
       private: true
       ---
       Gets the default drop down option for Time_Of_Day.
    default_widget (include_now:Boolean=False) -> Widget =
        options = [Option "<Fixed Time>" "Time_Of_Day.new"] + (if include_now then [Option "<Now>" "Time_Of_Day.now"] else [])
        Widget.Single_Choice values=options

    ## ---
       aliases: [greater than]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this time of day is later than `that` time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The time of day to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A time later than another
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45
       other = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       result = time > other
       # → True
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no time picker. Connect a time of day, or type one such
       as `Time_Of_Day.new 9`.
    > self that = Comparable.> self=self that

    ## ---
       aliases: [greater than or equal]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this time of day is the same as, or later than, `that` time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The time of day to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A time compared with itself
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45
       other = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45
       result = time >= other
       # → True
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no time picker. Connect a time of day, or type one such
       as `Time_Of_Day.new 9`.
    >= self that = Comparable.>= self=self that

    ## ---
       aliases: [less than]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this time of day is earlier than `that` time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The time of day to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A time earlier than another
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       other = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45
       result = time < other
       # → True
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no time picker. Connect a time of day, or type one such
       as `Time_Of_Day.new 9`.
    < self that = Comparable.< self=self that

    ## ---
       aliases: [less than or equal]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this time of day is the same as, or earlier than, `that` time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The time of day to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A time later than another
       ```
       time = Time_Of_Day.new 13 45
       other = Time_Of_Day.new 9
       result = time <= other
       # → False
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no time picker. Connect a time of day, or type one such
       as `Time_Of_Day.new 9`.
    <= self that = Comparable.<= self=self that

## ---
   private: true
   ---
   The choices for an amount of time added to, or taken from, a Time_Of_Day:
   each `Time_Period`, written autoscoped (`..Hour`), and `Duration.new` for a
   custom number of hours, minutes and seconds. `..Hour` resolves against the
   `Duration | Time_Period` union: the engine tries each member in turn, and
   only `Time_Period` has an `Hour` constructor (as `Column.date_part`'s
   `Date_Period | Time_Period = ..Day` default does).
make_time_period_selector -> Widget =
    periods = ["Hour", "Minute", "Second", "Millisecond", "Microsecond", "Nanosecond"].map name->
        code = ".." + name
        Option code code
    Widget.Single_Choice values=(periods + [Option "Duration.new" "(Duration.new)"])

## ---
   private: true
   ---
Time_Of_Day.from (that:JS_Object) =
    ## Must have hour and minute but second and nanosecond are optional
    case that.get "type" == "Time_Of_Day" && ["hour", "minute"].all that.contains_key of
        True ->
            if that.contains_key "nanosecond" && (that.contains_key "second" . not) then Error.throw (Illegal_Argument.Error "Invalid JS_Object for Time_Of_Day.from.") else
                Time_Of_Day.new (that.get "hour") (that.get "minute") (that.get "second" 0) nanosecond=(that.get "nanosecond" 0)
        False -> Error.throw (Illegal_Argument.Error "Invalid JS_Object for Time_Of_Day.from.")
